{"task_id": "Mbpp/6", "solution": "```python\ndef differ_At_One_Bit_Pos(number1: int, number2: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if two integers differ by exactly one bit in their binary representations.\n    Returns True if they differ by one bit, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    xor_result = number1 ^ number2\n    # A number has exactly one bit set if it is a power of two and non-zero.\n    return (xor_result > 0) and ((xor_result & (xor_result - 1)) == 0)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/3", "solution": "```python\ndef is_not_prime(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is non-prime. Returns True for non-primes, False for primes.\"\"\"\n    if number <= 1:\n        return False\n    if number == 2 or number == 3:\n        return False\n\n    if number % 2 == 0:\n        return True\n\n    # Check for odd divisors from 3 up to the square root of number\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= number:\n        if number % i == 0:\n            return True\n        i += 2\n\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/2", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Union, List, Tuple, Set, Any\n\ndef similar_elements(collection1: Union[List[Any], Tuple[Any]], collection2: Union[List[Any], Tuple[Any]]) -> Set[Union[int, float]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the shared numeric elements from two given collections (lists or tuples).\n    Non-numeric elements are ignored; a set of common numeric elements is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    set1 = {item for item in collection1 if isinstance(item, (int, float))}\n    set2 = {item for item in collection2 if isinstance(item, (int, float))}\n    common_elements = set1.intersection(set2)\n    return common_elements\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/3", "solution": "```python\ndef is_not_prime(number: int) -> bool:\n    # Numbers less than or equal to 1 are not considered non-prime.\n    if number <= 1:\n        return False\n    # The number 2 is a prime number, thus it is not non-prime.\n    if number == 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the square root of the number to find divisors.\n    # If a divisor is found, the number is non-prime and True is returned.\n    i = 2\n    while i * i <= number:\n        if number % i == 0:\n            return True\n        i += 1\n\n    # If no divisors are found, the number is prime and False is returned.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/4", "solution": "```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef heap_queue_largest(numbers: list[int], n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n largest integers from a list and returns them in descending order.\n    Uses a min-heap for efficient identification of largest elements.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Edge Case 1: n is 0 or numbers list is empty\n    if n == 0 or not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    # Edge Case 2: n is greater than or equal to the list size\n    if n >= len(numbers):\n        # Collect all elements, sort descending, and return.\n        return sorted(numbers, reverse=True)\n\n    # Standard Case: 0 < n < len(numbers)\n    # Use a min-heap to keep track of the n largest elements seen so far.\n    min_heap = []\n    for num in numbers:\n        if len(min_heap) < n:\n            heapq.heappush(min_heap, num)\n        elif num > min_heap[0]:\n            # If current number is larger than the smallest in the heap,\n            # remove the smallest and add the current number.\n            heapq.heappop(min_heap)\n            heapq.heappush(min_heap, num)\n\n    # The min_heap now contains the n largest numbers.\n    # Sort them in descending order before returning.\n    return sorted(min_heap, reverse=True)\n```\n"}
