{"task_id": "Mbpp/56", "solution": "def check(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given number is one less than twice its reverse.\n    For example:\n    - If number is 23, its reverse is 32. Twice its reverse is 64. One less than twice its reverse is 63. 23 != 63.\n    - If number is 1, its reverse is 1. Twice its reverse is 2. One less than twice its reverse is 1. 1 == 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    reversed_number_str = str(number)[::-1]\n    reversed_number = int(reversed_number_str)\n    return number == (2 * reversed_number) - 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/20", "solution": "```python\ndef is_woodall(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given integer is a Woodall number.\n    It iterates through non-negative 'n' to calculate candidates based on n * 2^n - 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < -1:\n        return False\n\n    n = 0\n    while True:\n        # Calculate the Woodall number candidate for the current 'n'\n        woodall_candidate = n * (2**n) - 1\n\n        if woodall_candidate == number:\n            return True\n        elif woodall_candidate > number:\n            # Since Woodall numbers are strictly increasing for n >= 0,\n            # if the candidate exceeds the number, no further 'n' will match.\n            return False\n\n        n += 1\n\n```"}
