{"task_id": "Mbpp/109", "solution": "```python\ndef _rotate_string(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Rotates a binary string once to the left.\n    Example: \"011001\" -> \"110010\"\n    \"\"\"\n    if not s:\n        return s\n    return s[1:] + s[0]\n\ndef _is_odd_binary(binary_str: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a binary string represents an odd decimal value.\n    It is odd if its least significant bit (rightmost character) is '1'.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not binary_str:\n        return False\n    return binary_str[-1] == '1'\n\ndef odd_Equivalent(binary_string: str, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts how many rotations of a binary string result in an odd decimal value.\n    It iterates through the specified number of rotations and checks each resulting string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not binary_string:\n        return 0\n\n    odd_count = 0\n    current_binary_string = binary_string\n\n    for _ in range(n):\n        if _is_odd_binary(current_binary_string):\n            odd_count += 1\n\n        if _ < n - 1:\n            current_binary_string = _rotate_string(current_binary_string)\n\n    return odd_count\n```"}
