{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "```python\ndef search(arr: list[int | float]) -> int | float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the element that appears exactly once in a sorted array.\n    All other elements in the array are assumed to appear an even number of times.\n    \"\"\"\n    low = 0\n    high = len(arr) - 1\n\n    while low < high:\n        mid = low + (high - low) // 2\n\n        # Adjust mid to be at an even index if it's currently odd\n        # This ensures we always compare an element at an even index with the one following it.\n        # Or an element at an odd index with the one preceding it.\n        # The logic below handles this implicitly:\n        # If mid is even, we compare arr[mid] with arr[mid+1].\n        # If mid is odd, we compare arr[mid-1] with arr[mid].\n        # The goal is that (mid, mid+1) or (mid-1, mid) form a pair.\n\n        if mid % 2 == 0:  # mid is at an even index\n            if arr[mid] == arr[mid + 1]:\n                # Pair found, unique element is to the right\n                low = mid + 2\n            else:\n                # Pair not found, unique element is at mid or to its left\n                high = mid\n        else:  # mid is at an odd index\n            if arr[mid] == arr[mid - 1]:\n                # Pair found, unique element is to the right\n                low = mid + 1\n            else:\n                # Pair not found, unique element is at mid-1 or to its left\n                # (since arr[mid] and arr[mid-1] don't match, arr[mid-1] could be unique)\n                high = mid - 1\n\n    return arr[low]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "def search(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the unique element in a sorted array where all other elements appear twice.\n    Uses a modified binary search to efficiently locate the single unique integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    low, high = 0, len(arr) - 1\n\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n\n        # Ensure mid always points to the start of a potential pair\n        # If mid is odd, make it even by decrementing\n        if mid % 2 == 1:\n            mid -= 1\n\n        # Compare arr[mid] with arr[mid+1]\n        if arr[mid] == arr[mid+1]:\n            # If they are equal, the unique element is to the right\n            low = mid + 2\n        else:\n            # If they are not equal, the unique element is arr[mid] or to its left\n            high = mid\n\n    return arr[low]"}
