{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef next_Perfect_Square(input_number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the smallest perfect square strictly greater than the input_number.\n    For negative inputs, it returns 0 as the smallest perfect square.\n    \"\"\"\n    if input_number < 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # Calculate the square root, then take its floor.\n    # For example, for 35, sqrt is ~5.91, floor is 5.\n    current_root_floor = math.floor(math.sqrt(input_number))\n\n    # The next integer whose square is strictly greater than input_number\n    # will be (current_root_floor + 1).\n    # For 35, next_root is 5 + 1 = 6.\n    next_root = current_root_floor + 1\n\n    return next_root * next_root\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "```python\ndef lps(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence of the given string.\n    It uses dynamic programming to build up the solution from smaller substrings.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - length + 1):\n            j = i + length - 1\n            if s[i] == s[j]:\n                if length == 2:\n                    dp[i][j] = 2\n                else:\n                    dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n\n    return dp[0][n - 1]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "```python\ndef lps(input_string: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string.\n    Uses dynamic programming for efficient computation.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(input_string)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the longest palindromic subsequence of input_string[i...j]\n    # Initialize all values to 0.\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # All single characters are palindromic subsequences of length 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the DP table for substrings of length 2 to n.\n    # 'current_length' is the length of the substring being considered.\n    for current_length in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - current_length + 1):\n            j = i + current_length - 1  # 'j' is the end index of the current substring\n\n            if input_string[i] == input_string[j]:\n                # If characters match, they extend the palindrome from the inner substring.\n                # dp[i+1][j-1] is 0 if the inner substring is empty (e.g., current_length is 2).\n                dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                # If characters don't match, take the maximum from excluding either character.\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n\n    return dp[0][n - 1]\n```"}
