{"task_id": "Mbpp/299", "solution": "```python\ndef _aggregate_scores(data: list[tuple[str, int]]) -> dict[str, int]:\n    \"\"\"Aggregates scores for each unique name from a list of name-score tuples.\n    It returns a dictionary where keys are names and values are their total scores.\"\"\"\n    aggregated_scores = {}\n    for name, score in data:\n        aggregated_scores[name] = aggregated_scores.get(name, 0) + score\n    return aggregated_scores\n\ndef _find_max_score(aggregated_scores: dict[str, int]) -> tuple[str, int]:\n    \"\"\"Finds the name with the highest score and that score from an aggregated scores dictionary.\n    This function assumes the input dictionary is not empty.\"\"\"\n    # Using max() directly is the simplest approach given constraints to trust inputs\n    # and not add explicit checks for edge cases like an empty dictionary.\n    max_name, max_score = max(aggregated_scores.items(), key=lambda item: item[1])\n    return (max_name, max_score)\n\ndef max_aggregate(data: list[tuple[str, int]]) -> tuple[str, int]:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the maximum aggregate score from a list of name-score tuples.\n    It aggregates scores by name and then finds the name with the highest total score.\"\"\"\n    aggregated_scores = _aggregate_scores(data)\n    max_name, max_score = _find_max_score(aggregated_scores)\n    return (max_name, max_score)\n```"}
