{"task_id": "Mbpp/440", "solution": "```python\nimport re\nfrom typing import Optional, Tuple\n\n# A predefined set of common adverbs for efficient lookup.\n# This list covers many typical adverbs, aiding in accurate identification (NFR1).\n_ADVERB_LEXICON = {\n    \"always\", \"usually\", \"often\", \"sometimes\", \"rarely\", \"never\",\n    \"very\", \"really\", \"quite\", \"just\", \"almost\", \"already\", \"still\", \"yet\",\n    \"quickly\", \"slowly\", \"carefully\", \"happily\", \"sadly\", \"clearly\",\n    \"here\", \"there\", \"everywhere\", \"nowhere\", \"anywhere\", \"somewhere\",\n    \"now\", \"then\", \"today\", \"yesterday\", \"tomorrow\", \"soon\", \"late\", \"early\",\n    \"well\", \"badly\", \"fast\", \"hard\", \"high\", \"low\", \"straight\", \"daily\", \"weekly\", \"monthly\", \"yearly\",\n    \"ago\", \"back\", \"forth\", \"in\", \"out\", \"up\", \"down\", \"away\", \"home\",\n    \"indeed\", \"perhaps\", \"probably\", \"possibly\", \"certainly\", \"definitely\", \"surely\",\n    \"consequently\", \"therefore\", \"thus\", \"however\", \"moreover\", \"furthermore\", \"nonetheless\",\n    \"much\", \"little\", \"enough\", \"too\", \"so\", \"rather\", \"scarcely\", \"hardly\", \"only\", \"even\", \"also\", \"either\", \"instead\"\n}\n\n\ndef find_adverb_position(sentence: str) -> Optional[Tuple[int, int, str]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies the first adverb in a sentence and returns its position and string.\n    Handles punctuation attached to words and uses a lexicon combined with an \"-ly\" rule.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Iterate through all word-like sequences in the sentence, preserving their original indices.\n    # The regex '\\\\b\\\\w+\\\\b' matches sequences of word characters and naturally handles leading/trailing punctuation by identifying word boundaries (NFR2).\n    for match in re.finditer(r'\\b\\w+\\b', sentence):\n        word = match.group(0)\n        start_index = match.start(0)\n        end_index = match.end(0)\n        word_lower = word.lower()\n\n        # Check if the extracted word is an adverb (FR2, NFR1).\n        # This is determined by checking against the lexicon or applying a common \"-ly\" suffix rule.\n        if word_lower in _ADVERB_LEXICON or (word_lower.endswith(\"ly\") and len(word_lower) > 3):\n            return (start_index, end_index, word)  # Return the adverb and its indices (FR3).\n\n    # If the loop completes without finding any adverbs, return None (FR4).\n    return None\n```"}
