{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "```python\ndef max_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum product of an increasing subsequence within the input array.\n    Uses dynamic programming to find the optimal product.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i] stores the max product of an increasing subsequence ending at arr[i]\n    dp = list(arr)\n\n    for i in range(len(arr)):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[j] < arr[i]:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] * arr[i])\n\n    return max(dp)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/470", "solution": "```python\ndef add_pairwise(input_tuple: tuple[int | float, ...]) -> tuple[int | float, ...]:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the pairwise sum of neighboring elements in a tuple.\n    Returns an empty tuple if the input_tuple has less than two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(input_tuple) < 2:\n        return ()\n\n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(input_tuple) - 1):\n        result.append(input_tuple[i] + input_tuple[i+1])\n    return tuple(result)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef max_product(numbers: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum product of an increasing subsequence within the given array.\n    This uses dynamic programming to efficiently find the max product ending at each index.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(numbers)\n\n    # If the input list is empty, max() on an empty list would raise ValueError.\n    # As per critical requirements, explicit checks for edge cases are forbidden,\n    # trusting the caller's input to be valid (e.g., non-empty, positive integers).\n    # If a non-empty list is guaranteed, the following logic naturally handles it.\n\n    # dp[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    dp = list(numbers)\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if numbers[i] > numbers[j]:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] * numbers[i])\n\n    # The maximum overall product is the maximum value found in the dp array.\n    return max(dp)\n```"}
