{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef check_Consecutive(listOfIntegers: List[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a list of integers forms a consecutive sequence after sorting and ensuring uniqueness.\n    Returns True if all elements are unique and consecutive; False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(listOfIntegers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    sorted_list = sorted(listOfIntegers)\n\n    # Validate that there are no duplicates in the sorted list.\n    for i in range(1, len(sorted_list)):\n        if sorted_list[i] == sorted_list[i-1]:\n            return False\n\n    # Verify that each element is exactly one greater than its predecessor.\n    for i in range(1, len(sorted_list)):\n        if sorted_list[i] != sorted_list[i-1] + 1:\n            return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "```python\ndef check_Consecutive(listOfNumbers: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the distinct, sorted numbers in a list form a consecutive sequence.\n    Returns True for empty or single-element lists.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not listOfNumbers or len(listOfNumbers) == 1:\n        return True\n\n    # Get distinct numbers and sort them\n    distinct_numbers = sorted(list(set(listOfNumbers)))\n\n    # Check if the sorted distinct numbers are consecutive\n    for i in range(len(distinct_numbers) - 1):\n        if distinct_numbers[i+1] != distinct_numbers[i] + 1:\n            return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "def _normalize_tuple(t: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Transforms a two-element tuple into a canonical form (min, max) for symmetric comparison.\"\"\"\n    return tuple(sorted(t))\n\ndef tuple_intersection(list1: list[tuple], list2: list[tuple]) -> set[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the intersection of two lists of tuples, treating (a, b) and (b, a) as identical.\n    It returns a set of normalized (sorted) tuples found in both input lists.\n    \"\"\"\n    normalized_set1 = {_normalize_tuple(t) for t in list1}\n    normalized_set2 = {_normalize_tuple(t) for t in list2}\n    return normalized_set1.intersection(normalized_set2)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/471", "solution": "```python\ndef find_remainder(arr: list[int], n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the product of list elements modulo `n`, preventing overflow by intermediate modulo operations.\n    Handles empty lists (product 1) and `n=1` (returns 0).\n    \"\"\"\n    # If n is 1, the remainder is always 0.\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n\n    # Initialize product to 1 for an empty list or first multiplication.\n    # Apply modulo n during each multiplication to prevent overflow.\n    product = 1\n    for element in arr:\n        product = (product * element) % n\n\n    return product\n```"}
