{"task_id": "Mbpp/564", "solution": "```python\ndef count_Pairs(input_list: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts unique unordered pairs with unequal elements from a list of integers.\n    Pairs are identified by iterating through the list and storing unequal pairs as frozensets in a set.\n    \"\"\"\n    unique_unequal_pairs = set()\n    n = len(input_list)\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):  # Iterate j from i + 1 to ensure unique unordered pairs\n            element_a = input_list[i]\n            element_b = input_list[j]\n\n            if element_a != element_b:\n                # Use frozenset to represent an unordered pair, ensuring uniqueness in the main set.\n                unique_unequal_pairs.add(frozenset({element_a, element_b}))\n\n    return len(unique_unequal_pairs)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/564", "solution": "```python\ndef count_Pairs(input_list: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of possible unordered pairs of distinct elements within the input list.\n    This implementation adheres to the design specification for handling duplicates and pair identification.\n    \"\"\"\n    # If the list has fewer than two elements, no pairs can be formed.\n    if len(input_list) < 2:\n        return 0\n\n    # Extract unique elements from the input list to ensure distinctness for pairs.\n    # Sorting is done for consistent iteration but is not strictly necessary for correctness.\n    unique_elements = sorted(list(set(input_list)))\n\n    # A set to store the identified distinct unordered pairs.\n    # frozenset is used for pairs as it is immutable and hashable, suitable for set elements.\n    distinct_pairs = set()\n\n    # Iterate through the unique elements to form all unique unordered pairs.\n    # The nested loops ensure each pair {a, b} is considered once where a != b.\\n    num_unique = len(unique_elements)\n    for i in range(num_unique):\n        for j in range(i + 1, num_unique):\n            pair = frozenset({unique_elements[i], unique_elements[j]})\n            distinct_pairs.add(pair)\n\n    # The total count of distinct unordered pairs is the size of the set.\n    return len(distinct_pairs)\n```"}
