{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\n\ndef perfect_squares(num1, num2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies perfect squares within a specified integer range [num1, num2] inclusive.\n    Returns an empty list if num1 > num2 or no perfect squares are found.\n    \"\"\"\n    if num1 > num2:\n        return []\n\n    # Determine the smallest integer k whose square is >= num1 (and non-negative).\n    # max(0, num1) ensures the square root is taken of a non-negative number.\n    k_start = math.ceil(math.sqrt(max(0, num1)))\n\n    # Determine the largest integer k whose square is <= num2.\n    # max(0, num2) ensures the square root is taken of a non-negative number,\n    # correctly handling cases where num2 might be negative.\n    k_end = math.floor(math.sqrt(max(0, num2)))\n\n    results = []\n    # Iterate from k_start to k_end (inclusive) to generate perfect squares.\n    for k in range(int(k_start), int(k_end) + 1):\n        square = k * k\n        results.append(square)\n\n    return results\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef perfect_squares(start: int, end: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds perfect squares within an inclusive range [start, end].\n    It iterates through numbers and checks if their square root is an integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    for num in range(start, end + 1):\n        if num < 0:\n            continue # Perfect squares are defined as non-negative integers.\n\n        # Calculate the integer part of the square root.\n        sqrt_num = int(math.sqrt(num))\n        # Verify if num is a perfect square by squaring the integer root.\n        if sqrt_num * sqrt_num == num:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n```"}
