{"task_id": "Mbpp/603", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef _is_prime(num: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime using an efficient primality test.\n    It handles edge cases for 1, 2, and even numbers greater than 2.\"\"\"\n    if num <= 1:\n        return False\n    if num == 2:\n        return True\n    if num % 2 == 0: # All other even numbers are not prime\n        return False\n    # Iterate from 3 up to the square root of num, checking only odd divisors.\n    for i in range(3, int(math.sqrt(num)) + 1, 2):\n        if num % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef get_ludic(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Generates a sorted list of lucid numbers up to n, including 1 and all primes.\n    The list is inherently sorted as numbers are added in ascending order.\"\"\"\n    lucid_numbers_list = [1]\n    for num in range(2, n + 1):\n        if _is_prime(num):\n            lucid_numbers_list.append(num)\n    return lucid_numbers_list\n```"}
