{"task_id": "Mbpp/635", "solution": "```python\n# Helper function to maintain the heap property in a subtree rooted at index i.\n# n is the size of the heap.\ndef _heapify(arr: list, n: int, i: int) -> None:\n    largest = i\n    left_child = 2 * i + 1\n    right_child = 2 * i + 2\n\n    if left_child < n and arr[left_child] > arr[largest]:\n        largest = left_child\n\n    if right_child < n and arr[right_child] > arr[largest]:\n        largest = right_child\n\n    if largest != i:\n        arr[i], arr[largest] = arr[largest], arr[i]  # Swap\n        _heapify(arr, n, largest) # Recursively heapify the affected sub-tree\n\n# Helper function to build a max-heap from an array.\ndef _build_max_heap(arr: list) -> None:\n    n = len(arr)\n    # Start from the last non-leaf node and heapify upwards.\n    for i in range(n // 2 - 1, -1, -1):\\\n        _heapify(arr, n, i)\n\ndef heap_sort(input_list: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Sorts a list using the heap sort algorithm in-place and returns the sorted list.\"\"\"\n    n = len(input_list)\n\n    _build_max_heap(input_list)\n\n    # Extract elements one by one from the heap.\n    for i in range(n - 1, 0, -1):\\\n        input_list[i], input_list[0] = input_list[0], input_list[i]  # Move current root to end\n        _heapify(input_list, i, 0) # Heapify the reduced heap (size 'i')\n\n    return input_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/633", "solution": "```python\ndef pair_xor_Sum(numbers: list, length: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of XOR operations for all unique pairs in a list.\"\"\"\n    total_xor_sum = 0\n    for i in range(length):\n        for j in range(i + 1, length):\n            total_xor_sum += (numbers[i] ^ numbers[j])\n    return total_xor_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef is_divisible_pair(a: int, b: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if two integers are mutually divisible, handling zero carefully.\n    A non-zero number is not considered mutually divisible with zero due to division by zero being undefined.\n    Thus, zero cannot form a mutually divisible pair with any non-zero number.\n    \"\"\"\n    if a == 0 or b == 0:\n        # If one number is zero and the other is non-zero, they are not mutually divisible.\n        # If both are zero, 0 % 0 is undefined, so they are not considered mutually divisible in this context.\n        return False\n\n    return a % b == 0 or b % a == 0\n\ndef is_divisible_subset(subset: List[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if all pairs within a given subset are mutually divisible.\"\"\"\n    if len(subset) <= 1:\n        return True\n    for i in range(len(subset)):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(subset)):\n            if not is_divisible_pair(subset[i], subset[j]):\n                return False\n    return True\n\ndef largest_subset(numbers: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the size of the largest subset where every pair of numbers is mutually divisible.\"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n\n    # Sort the numbers as an internal optimization, as indicated in the design.\n    # This helps with consistent subset generation and processing.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    max_size = 0\n    n = len(sorted_numbers)\n\n    # Generate all subsets using bit manipulation.\n    # This corresponds to the \"Generates potential subsets\" step in the sequence diagram.\n    for i in range(1 << n):  # Iterate from 0 to 2^n - 1 to represent all possible subsets\n        current_subset = []\n        for j in range(n):\n            if (i >> j) & 1:  # Check if the j-th bit is set in 'i'\n                current_subset.append(sorted_numbers[j])\n\n        # Check if the current_subset is a divisible subset.\n        # This maps to the \"is_divisible_subset(subset: List[int])\" step.\n        if is_divisible_subset(current_subset):\n            if len(current_subset) > max_size:\n                max_size = len(current_subset)\n\n    return max_size\n```"}
