{"task_id": "Mbpp/805", "solution": "```python\ndef max_sum_list(list_of_lists: list[list[int]]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Identifies and returns the inner list from a list of lists whose sum of elements is the highest.\n    Returns an empty list if the input list of lists is empty or contains only empty lists with non-positive sums.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not list_of_lists:\n        return []\n\n    max_sum = -float('inf')\n    result_list = []\n\n    for inner_list in list_of_lists:\n        current_sum = sum(inner_list)\n        if current_sum > max_sum:\n            max_sum = current_sum\n            result_list = inner_list\n\n    # Handle the case where all sums are negative or zero and result_list might still be empty if max_sum was never updated from -inf\n    # This specifically addresses the requirement \"Returns an empty list ([]) if ... contains only empty lists and no positive sums.\"\n    # If all lists are empty, `result_list` would become `[]` (from first empty list), and `max_sum` would be `0`. Returns `[]`.\n    # If all lists have negative sums, `result_list` would be the one with the highest negative sum.\n    # The condition `if current_sum > max_sum:` correctly handles both cases as per the sequence diagram.\n    # For example, `max_sum_list([[], [], []])` -> `result_list` becomes `[]`, `max_sum` becomes `0`. Returns `[]`.\n    # `max_sum_list([[-1], [-2]])` -> `result_list` becomes `[-1]`, `max_sum` becomes `-1`. Returns `[-1]`.\n    # The initial `result_list = []` covers the case where the highest sum is 0 (from empty list) if there are no positive sums.\n    # The only scenario where `result_list` might remain `[]` would be if `list_of_lists` was empty, which is handled at the beginning.\n\n    return result_list\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/462", "solution": "```python\ndef combinations_list(input_list: list) -> list[list]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates all possible combinations of elements from an input list, including the empty list.\n    The order of combinations in the output matches the specified example.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = [[]]  # Start with the empty list as the initial combination.\n\n    for element in input_list:\n        # Create new combinations by prepending the current element to all existing combinations.\n        new_combinations_this_iteration = []\n        for existing_combination in result:\n            new_combination = [element] + existing_combination\n            new_combinations_this_iteration.append(new_combination)\n        result.extend(new_combinations_this_iteration)\n\n    return result\n```"}
